r of vertices. This implies a vertex with value 0 and another with value 2.  Consequently, all vertices with value 0 must be adjacent to a vertex with value 2.\\n\\nExploring how to bound the radius with `romanWeight f`. Looking at prior proofs related to ball p\n<truncated 2365 bytes>\np_index\":1085,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"RUNNING\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:29:20Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:29:20+02:00\\nTool is running as a background task with task id: f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/task-1085\\nTask Description: /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -u -c '\\nimport networkx as nx\\nimport itertools\\n\\natlas = nx.graph_atlas_g()\\n\\nchecked = 0\\nfound_u_count = 0\\nfor G in atlas:\\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\\n    if n < 2 or not nx.is_connected(G): continue\\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\\n    for f_tuple in itertools.product([0, 1, 2], repeat=n):\\n        f = dict(zip(nodes, f_tuple))\\n        valid = True\\n        for v in nodes:\\n            if f[v] == 0:\\n                if not any(f[u] == 2 for u in G.neighbors(v)):\\n                    valid = False\\n                    break\\n        if valid:\\n            w = sum(f_tuple)\\n            # check if there exists u in nodes with ecc(u) <= w - 1\\n            # or even u in S (where f(u) == 2)?\\n            eccs = nx.eccentricity(G)\\n            rad = nx.radius(G)\\n            # does there exist any node with ecc <= w - 1?\\n            # since rad = min(ecc), this is literally equivalent to rad <= w - 1!\\n            if rad > w - 1:\\n                print(f\\\"NO! rad={rad}, w={w}\\\")\\n                exit(0)\\n    checked += 1\\nprint(f\\\"Checked {checked} graphs. In 100% of cases, rad <= w - 1!\\\")\\n'\\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/tasks/task-1085.log\\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO